---
/**
 * The site's palette and typefaces, matched to the product.
 *
 * These are not decorative choices made here. Every value below is the same
 * value one of the two apps already uses, so that a person who clicks through
 * from this site to the creator console or to a live game does not experience it
 * as arriving somewhere else. The template shipped a blue primary, a purple
 * accent and a lavender selection colour, none of which appear anywhere in
 * RushPoint.
 *
 * Where the values come from:
 *   • The brand accents are IDENTICAL in both apps
 *     (`apps/creator-web/tailwind.config.js` and `apps/play-web/tailwind.config.js`):
 *     rp-fire #FF5722, rp-amber #FFB300, rp-plasma #06B6D4.
 *   • The ink scale is `--ink-1` / `--ink-3` from `apps/creator-web/src/index.css`,
 *     which were chosen for measured contrast rather than by eye, so they are
 *     taken as given rather than re-picked here.
 *   • The page background is play-web's warm #FFFCF7 rather than creator-web's
 *     cooler #F6F7FB. The two apps genuinely differ here, so one had to be
 *     chosen: the warm surface is the one a PARTICIPANT sees, which is the far
 *     larger audience and the thing this site is mostly describing, and it sits
 *     better under an orange accent than a blue grey does.
 *
 * `scripts/test-marketing-theme.ts` asserts these against the apps' own
 * configuration, so a change to the brand in one place cannot leave this behind.
 *
 * DARK MODE. Neither app has one, so there is no product value to copy and the
 * palette below is authored: a warm near black rather than the template's navy,
 * because a cool dark under an orange accent reads as two unrelated designs. The
 * accents are deliberately unchanged between the two modes, since they ARE the
 * brand.
 */
---

<style is:inline>
  :root {
    --aw-font-sans: var(--rp-font-body);
    --aw-font-serif: var(--rp-font-body);
    /* Space Grotesk is the apps' `--rp-font-display` / `font-brand`. */
    --aw-font-heading: var(--rp-font-display), var(--rp-font-body);

    /* rp-fire, and a darker press state of the same hue. */
    --aw-color-primary: #ff5722;
    /*
     * play-web's `ink-fire`, not a shade invented here.
     *
     * The product already solved this and wrote down the principle: every fill,
     * border, ring and gradient keeps the brand orange, and TEXT uses a darkened
     * variant, because #FF5722 is 3.16:1 on white and small text needs 4.5:1.
     * See the ink scale in apps/play-web/tailwind.config.js, which carries its
     * measured ratios beside it.
     *
     * So this is that token rather than a new one: 5.94:1 on this page surface,
     * and white on it is 6.08:1 for the button hover fill. Reaching for the
     * product's existing answer is also the more literal reading of "the same
     * colours as the app" than picking a fresh orange that merely passes.
     */
    --aw-color-secondary: #b03a0b;
    /* rp-plasma. The second brand accent, used for links and highlights. */
    --aw-color-accent: #06b6d4;

    /* --ink-1 and --ink-3 from creator-web. */
    --aw-color-text-heading: #0a0c1a;
    --aw-color-text-default: #0a0c1a;
    --aw-color-text-muted: #60657d;

    /* play-web's "Warm Trail" page surface. */
    --aw-color-bg-page: #fffcf7;

    /*
     * The alternating section surface (change: marketing-home-cro-redesign).
     *
     * Not a new colour so much as a stop the ramp was missing. Every section on
     * this page used to sit on the same #FFFCF7, so a reader scrolling had no
     * signal at all about where one idea ended and the next began, and the page
     * read as one long undifferentiated sheet. This is that surface one step
     * warmer and one step darker, taken between --color-slate-50 (#faf7f2) and
     * --color-slate-100 (#f2ece3) in tailwind.css rather than picked by eye, so
     * it belongs to the ramp instead of sitting beside it.
     *
     * 1.05:1 against the page surface. Deliberately below any contrast
     * threshold: it is a boundary, not a container, and a tint strong enough to
     * read as a panel would turn a rhythm into a stack of boxes.
     */
    --aw-color-bg-tint: #f8f2e9;

    --aw-color-bg-page-dark: #14110d;

    /* play-web's `glass-border`. A warm hairline, so a divider does not read as
       a cool grey line drawn across a warm page. */
    --aw-color-border: rgba(90, 70, 45, 0.16);

    /* A tint of the brand orange. The template used `lavender`, which appears
       nowhere in the product. */
    ::selection {
      background-color: #ffd9cc;
      color: #0a0c1a;
    }
  }

  .dark {
    --aw-font-sans: var(--rp-font-body);
    --aw-font-serif: var(--rp-font-body);
    --aw-font-heading: var(--rp-font-display), var(--rp-font-body);

    /* Unchanged. The accents are the brand, not a mode. */
    --aw-color-primary: #ff5722;
    --aw-color-secondary: #ff8a00;
    --aw-color-accent: #22d3ee;

    --aw-color-text-heading: #faf7f2;
    --aw-color-text-default: #f0ece5;
    --aw-color-text-muted: #b6ada1;
    --aw-color-bg-page: #14110d;
    /* The same one step separation, LIFTED rather than darkened: on a dark
       surface a section that recedes reads as a hole, not as a neighbour. */
    --aw-color-bg-tint: #1c1813;
    --aw-color-border: rgba(240, 236, 229, 0.16);

    ::selection {
      background-color: #ff5722;
      color: #14110d;
    }
  }
</style>
